{
  "dom_id": "step-e2",
  "name": "Example: Finding a Limit When the Limit is Not Equal to the Function Value",
  "problem": "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0} f(x)$ for the function below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14198813/assets/c0ed78e2e640611d70263e17a76eb4a13e9e2816efba02494b53e540d38065d6.png)",
  "worked_solution": "As $x$ approaches $0,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)= 1\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(0)$ is $2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $1$.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
